Mars Fissure: walk, or fly, over a piece of Mars at full size. Shows the engine's terrains.

The ground is a HiRISE digital terrain model of a fissure east of Olympus Mons (DTEEC_046252_1975_046107_1975_A01, 17.2 N
233.3 E): 6244 x 12235 heights, 1.01 m apart, so 6.3 x 12.4 km, from 961 to 1263 m above Mars's datum. It is drawn and walked
on at 1:1, at a metre per sample near the camera, all the way to the horizon. Eight survey beacons are spread over it: one at the
bottom of the fissure, one on the highest ground, and six others. Find them all.

    mars_fissure                    walk from the rim of the fissure
    mars_fissure --fly              start flying
    mars_fissure --levels           colour the terrain's levels of detail
    mars_fissure --detail 600       draw full detail out to 600 m (300 is the default)
    mars_fissure --spawn 3000 9000  start 3 km east and 9 km north of the scan's south west corner
    mars_fissure --flythrough 60 syncmode=3
                                    fly a set route for 60 seconds, logging the frame times and what the terrain streamed, then
                                    quit (see src/flythrough.h). syncmode=3 turns vsync off

Controls:
    Mouse               look
    W A S D             walk (Shift: run, Space: jump. Mars's gravity is 38% of Earth's)
    F                   fly or walk. Flying: Space or E up, Q down, Shift 10 times faster (400 m/s), Ctrl 10 times slower
    R                   back to the start
    L                   colour the levels of detail
    K                   freeze the levels of detail where they are, then fly away to look at them
    [ ]                 less or more detail
    H                   hide the controls
    F10                 debug windows. Windows > Terrain shows what is drawn and streamed, and has the same settings
    F11                 full screen
    Escape              quit

THE CONTENT

    content/mars_terrain.gcpak  the terrain, baked from the DTM by gcpak_editor (309 MB)
    content/mars.gcpak          the ground's material, and the sky, made by mars_fissure_contentgen (contentgen/main.cpp)
    content/shaders.gcpak       as for the other games. It must have terrain.vert and terrain.frag in it:
                                    gcpak_editor --pack content/shader_src --out content/shaders.gcpak

The DTM isn't in the repository: it is 300 MB. Download DTEEC_046252_1975_046107_1975_A01.IMG from https://www.uahirise.org/dtm/
(or the PDS) and set the CMake variable MARS_DTM to where it is. The build then bakes it (the mars_fissure_terrain target):

    gcpak_editor --bake-terrain DTEEC_046252_1975_046107_1975_A01.IMG --out content/mars_terrain.gcpak --name mars_fissure.terrain

which takes a few seconds in a release build. It is baked again only if the DTM or the baker changes. Without MARS_DTM, the
target isn't made, and the game says so when it starts. The rest is made by the mars_fissure_content target whenever the content
generator or gcpak_editor changes:

    mars_fissure_contentgen content/mars.gcpak mars_sky.hdr
    gcpak_editor --pack mars_sky.hdr --out content/mars.gcpak --append

Nothing was photographed: the sky is a model of a dusty Martian sky (butterscotch, with a bluish glow round the sun), and the
ground is dust and pebbles made with noise. The DTM only has heights, so the colour of the ground is the same everywhere apart
from where it is steep (rock) and some large, faint blotches.

WHAT IT SHOWS

    Terrains            a TerrainComponent names a TERRAIN asset. The TerrainSystem chooses levels of detail for the camera
                        (CDLOD: detailed near, coarse far, turning smoothly from one into the next) and streams the tiles it needs
                        into a cache in GPU memory, from the mapped .gcpak file, on the render work thread. Only ~800 of the
                        terrain's 19,113 tiles are in GPU memory at once at walking speed, a few thousand when flying around. The
                        whole terrain is one instanced draw. See gamecore/include/gamecore/gc_terrain_system.h
    Collisions          the CollisionSystem collides with the terrain using the same tiles that are drawn near the camera, so the
                        explorer walks on exactly what is seen. Nothing is built when the game starts. See src/explorer.cpp
    The sky and haze    a SkyboxComponent lights the ground with the sky, and a FogComponent fades the distance (and the edge of
                        the scan) into it
    Point lights        the beacons' lamps light the ground around them

WHAT ISN'T THERE

No shadows: the engine's only shadows are baked into one shadow map per world, and a 12 km world would need one far too big to be
sharp. Cascaded shadow maps would suit terrain. The terrain has no texture of its own (HiRISE's orthoimages would give it one,
streamed with the tiles), so its colour doesn't vary as the real ground's does. The scan's edges are where the world ends: you
can walk off them, and are put back.
